WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:19.500
THE MOTOROLA DISASTER:
Remember Motorola?

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:24.740
At one point, they were selling network equipment,
signal amplifiers, and even digital video

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:25.740
recorders.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:31.320
But, likely the product that they’re most
well known for is cell phones and particularly

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.090
this one: the Motorola Razr.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:39.620
Back in the early 2000s, this phone was ubiquitous
and you can’t forget about the nostalgic

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:40.920
ringtone either.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.559
There was something about the sleek look of
the phone and the premium build that people

00:00:44.559 --> 00:00:46.590
just couldn’t get enough of.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:51.660
You could even argue that it was the status
symbol of the time given that it wasn’t

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:56.140
cheap having launched for $500 or the price
as the original iPhone.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:01.420
Nonetheless, Motorola went on to sell 130
million units during their hayday.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:07.700
To this day, the only iPhone that has sold
better is the iPhone 6 and 6+ as a combination.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:12.360
The bad part about the Razr for Motorola was
that it was basically their peak.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.950
While they had a substantial business outside
of cell phones, their cell phone business

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.250
was what was really iconic.

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:23.590
And as we saw the introduction of blackberries
and smartphones, Motorola’s phones quickly

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:24.850
fell out of favor.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:30.159
They tried to pivot to the smartphone market
themselves, but this wasn’t all that successful

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:31.571
and that brings us to early 2011.

00:01:31.571 --> 00:01:37.790
To most, it seemed obvious that Motorola was
on their way out, and that their fate was

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:39.350
similar to that of Blackberry.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:44.590
Yet, there was one person willing to take
a gamble on Motorola: Google.

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:51.190
In August of 2011, Google agreed to pay $12.5
billion in cash to acquire the waning company.

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:55.040
As you would guess, critics and investors
were rather skeptical with many predicting

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.650
that it would be a colossal disaster.

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:02.340
And as fate would have it, that’s exactly
what happened.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.190
Less than three years after the purchase,
Google would end up selling Motorola to Lenovo

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:08.479
for just $2.9 billion.

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:15.660
On paper, this was clearly a massive loss
having cost Google $9.6 billion plus the cost

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:17.360
of running Motorola.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:22.510
Time would declare that Motorola was a gargantuan
mistake that only Google could afford to make,

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:27.599
and there were several similar articles mocking
Google for their seemingly apparent incompetency.

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:32.190
But with time, people started to see a different
side of the picture and realized what game

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:33.970
Google was really playing.

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:38.290
So, here’s the story of Google’s “worst
acquisition” that actually turned out to

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:40.300
be their best.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:45.470
LAUNCHING ANDROID:
Hearing that Motorola may have been Google’s

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:48.770
best acquisition is probably hard to believe
at first glance.

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:53.400
After all, Google has had so many successful
acquisitions, and most people would point

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:58.150
to YouTube or Android as being Google’s
best acquisition especially given that both

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:00.940
of them cost substantially less than Motorola.

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:07.459
But, the thing to note is that Motorola actually
played a massive role in Android’s success.

00:03:07.459 --> 00:03:12.310
And to really understand this, we’ll have
to take a look back at origins of Android.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:17.020
The story starts in October of 2003 well before
the launch of the iPhone.

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:20.090
At the time, Android wasn’t even being developed
for smartphones.

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:23.090
It was actually being developed for digital
cameras.

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:27.860
But, the founders quickly realized that the
smartphone space had way more growth potential

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:31.920
and they would pivot which attracted the interest
of Larry Page of Google.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:36.239
Page could see that the mobile industry was
gaining a lot of steam, and he wanted Google

00:03:36.239 --> 00:03:37.540
to be very much involved.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:43.030
But, Google and Android had a major problem:
they didn’t have any hardware.

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:47.720
Neither of them had any experience building
any hardware and while they tried, it became

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:50.709
clear very early on that they wouldn’t be
able to compete with Apple.

00:03:50.709 --> 00:03:51.730
After the iPhone announcement, So, the focus
Android could surely not be about the underwhelming

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:52.810
Google phone.

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:56.650
Rather, the focus of Android had to be about
how it could power thousands of different

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:58.810
phones unlike Apple.

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:02.660
And to make this a reality, Google needed
phone manufacturers on board.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:06.370
Cue in the Open Handset Alliance in November
of 2007.

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:10.099
This alliance connected network operators,
software developers, component manufacturers,

00:04:10.099 --> 00:04:13.799
and device manufacturers who would all support
and embrace android.

00:04:13.799 --> 00:04:17.979
As you might’ve guessed, one of the device
manufacturers on board was Motorola.

00:04:17.979 --> 00:04:22.640
The Motorola Droid would become the first
phone that ran Android 2.0 out of the box,

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:24.980
but they were by no means the last.

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:29.450
All of Google’s partners would launch phone
after phone running Android, and given that

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:33.479
these phones were generally cheaper and more
customizable than their Apple counterparts,

00:04:33.479 --> 00:04:35.250
many would choose Android.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:40.130
And by 2010, Android would overtake iOS in
terms of total installations.

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:44.789
Meanwhile, Google was pumping out update after
update as there was simply so many features

00:04:44.789 --> 00:04:45.789
to add.

00:04:45.789 --> 00:04:49.930
And eventually, they would even come out with
their own smartphone: the Google Nexus.

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:54.759
But, while Android was a massive success in
terms of adoption and popularity, there were

00:04:54.759 --> 00:04:59.229
two massive clouds looming over the project:
Apple and Microsoft.

00:04:59.229 --> 00:05:03.949
These two had dominated the OS space for decades
on the computer side of things, and they very

00:05:03.949 --> 00:05:06.990
much wanted to extend this monopoly into mobile
space.

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:10.880
So, they would leverage all the patents that
they had accumulated over the decades and

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.870
start suing hell out of Google.

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:18.949
LEGAL ONSLAUGHT:
Google didn’t really have an easy way out

00:05:18.949 --> 00:05:20.449
of these lawsuits.

00:05:20.449 --> 00:05:25.720
The truth was that Google probably was infringing
on a bunch of patents from Apple and Microsoft.

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:29.289
And the fact that Android was open source
just made it easier for these companies to

00:05:29.289 --> 00:05:33.850
scover through the code and pinpoint infringements,
and that’s exactly what they did.

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:37.970
This quote from Steve Jobs is probably all
you need to understand Apple’s stance on

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:38.970
the situation.

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:44.440
“I will spend my last dying breath if I
need to, and I will spend every penny of Apple's

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:47.250
$40 billion in the bank, to right this wrong.

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:51.240
I'm going to destroy Android, because it's
a stolen product.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:54.160
I'm willing to go thermonuclear war on this."

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:57.860
While Microsoft approached the situation more
professionally, I’m sure they had similar

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:00.860
beliefs internally given that they would also
sue Google.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:05.220
At first, Google and their partners tried
to countersue Apple and Microsoft and win

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:06.220
in court.

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:08.720
After all, it’s not like they didn’t have
resources themselves.

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:13.930
But, this didn’t turn out all that favorably
for them as jurys would start ruling against

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:14.930
them.

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:17.160
Take Apple’s case against Samsung for example.

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:21.140
The jury ended up awarding Apple $1.049 billion
in damages.

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:26.180
Meanwhile, Samsung’s counter lawsuit was
awarded $0 in damages.

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:28.919
This was a massive win for Apple.

00:06:28.919 --> 00:06:32.590
Apple won more money from Samsung than Equifax
had to pay for leaking the social security

00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:34.930
numbers of a 140 million people.

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:39.910
That’s definitely quite messed up, but Google
wasn’t even worried about the financial

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:41.280
consequences.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:46.520
If Apple or Microsoft were willing to settle
for $5 or $10 billion, Google probably would’ve

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:47.610
taken the deal.

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:52.611
But, the problem was that Apple and Microsoft
didn’t care about the cash either, they

00:06:52.611 --> 00:06:54.569
had plenty of cash themselves.

00:06:54.569 --> 00:06:56.020
They were in it for the marketshare.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:59.419
They’re goal was to shut down Android by
suing Google.

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:04.120
And given that courts were starting to side
with Apple, this made Google very nervous.

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:09.419
Clearly, Steve Jobs wasn’t wiling to meet
in the middle, so Google only had one option:

00:07:09.419 --> 00:07:11.330
fight fire with fire.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:15.319
Instead of focusing on how they could defend
Android, Google would shift their focus onto

00:07:15.319 --> 00:07:17.030
how they could attack Apple.

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:20.400
And very quickly, one option became very clear.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:24.640
You see, while Apple was creating all this
commotion about Google ripping off iOS with

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:28.950
Android, they themselves had a history of
ripping off other companies and were dealing

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:30.879
with lawsuits themselves.

00:07:30.879 --> 00:07:34.740
One company that had a bunch of dirt on Apple
was Motorola.

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:39.330
Over the years, they had accumulated 17,000
patents relating to wireless communications,

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:43.830
networks, antennas, cellular communications,
and who knows what else.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:47.199
They also had 7,500 patents pending.

00:07:47.199 --> 00:07:52.629
On top of this, Motorola already suing Apple
on several fronts, and they were winning.

00:07:52.629 --> 00:07:56.690
One victory even allowed them to prevent Apple
from selling iPhones in Germany until they

00:07:56.690 --> 00:07:58.699
removed a wireless feature from the phone.

00:07:58.699 --> 00:08:06.080
For Google, this was a grand slam and they
were willing to pay any monetary price.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:11.379
DEFENDING ANDROID:
Motorola knew what Google was trying to do

00:08:11.379 --> 00:08:13.740
and they weren’t exactly against it.

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:17.789
The truth was, while they were pulling off
some victories against Apple, their business

00:08:17.789 --> 00:08:20.920
was dying and they were very much ready to
cash out.

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:24.169
They very much knew their worth though and
played hard ball with Google.

00:08:24.169 --> 00:08:29.389
At first, Google was looking to buy the company
for high $20 per share, but Motorola would

00:08:29.389 --> 00:08:31.610
work them up to $40 per share.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:36.740
This was way more than the business was worth,
and that’s why Google received so much heat

00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:37.740
for this.

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:42.000
But, as we discussed, Google couldn’t care
less about Motorola’s business, they were

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:45.840
interested in their intellectual property
and this is what they would keep.

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:50.850
When Google sold Motorola to Lenovo, they
would keep all but 2,000 patents.

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:55.400
And this is when Apple and Microsoft finally
realized that Google wasn’t gonna let go

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:56.690
either.

00:08:56.690 --> 00:09:00.640
Just three months after Google sold the remains
of Motorola, Apple would settle with Google,

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:04.760
and the duo would agree to drop all patent
litigations against each other.

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:09.350
Microsoft would hold out for quite a bit longer,
but they too would eventually come to their

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:10.350
senses.

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:15.579
If Google was able to just save Android by
flipping Motorolla for a $9.6 billion loss,

00:09:15.579 --> 00:09:19.529
that itself would’ve been worth it given
that Android is now the most popular operating

00:09:19.529 --> 00:09:24.250
system in the entire world, but it gets even
better for Google.

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:29.350
You see, $2.91 billion sale to Lenovo wasn’t
the only sale that Google was able to make

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:30.800
with Motorola.

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:36.279
They were also able to sell Motorola’s set-top
box unit to Arris for $2.35 billion reducing

00:09:36.279 --> 00:09:41.290
their total loss to $7.24 billion, but it
doesn’t stop right there either.

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:44.860
Right after Google bought Motorola, they were
able to rollover a bunch of Motorola’s previous

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:49.860
losses onto their financial statement yielding
them a tax benefit of $6.6 billion.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:54.480
So, really, Google’s total out of pocket
cost to acquire Motorola’s entire patent

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:58.790
library and defend Android was a mere $640
million.

00:09:58.790 --> 00:10:03.540
For perspective, Google made about $79 billion
in the last 12 month period.

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:08.120
So, the whole Motorola ordeal cost them just
3 days worth of net income.

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:13.450
Not to mention, they got to keep 22,500 patents
and applications which works out to less than

00:10:13.450 --> 00:10:15.360
$29,000 per patent.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:19.880
They can use these patents to not only develop
new devices and technologies, but they can

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:25.050
use them to defend the company and even go
on the offensive.

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:29.480
VICTORY:
In the end, while the Motorola acquisition

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:34.220
seemed stupid at the time given the company’s
fundamentals, there was clearly more to it

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:35.410
than just that.

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:39.620
Google brilliantly leveraged Motorola’s
extensive patent library to protect one of

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:42.100
their biggest assets: Android.

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:46.740
And they didn’t end up paying all that much
out of pocket either at the end of the day.

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:50.880
Critics argue that Google likely could’ve
defended Android even without Motorola.

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:56.139
While Apple was making some progress against
Android, it’s unlikely that Apple would’ve

00:10:56.139 --> 00:10:57.920
been able to shut down Android altogether.

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:03.070
So, maybe the better play was to just let
Apple play their games and eventually just

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:04.070
settle in court.

00:11:04.070 --> 00:11:07.260
But, that likely would’ve been a lot more
expensive.

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:11.769
If Apple was able to win $1 billion from Samsung,
imagine how much they might’ve been able

00:11:11.769 --> 00:11:13.730
to win against Google.

00:11:13.730 --> 00:11:18.130
Not to mention, Google wouldn’t have acquired
an arsenal of patents for dirt cheap prices

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:19.820
if they went that route.

00:11:19.820 --> 00:11:23.980
Now, you could definitely argue that Android
itself was a better acquisition because if

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:28.160
they hadn’t bought Android, there would’ve
been nothing to protect in the first place.

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:33.440
And while that is true, Motorola remains their
biggest acquisition to this day and it’s

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:36.550
by far their most underrated and strategic.

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:41.639
And that makes Motorola Google’s most interesting
acquisition for me, but that’s just what

00:11:41.639 --> 00:11:42.639
I think.

00:11:42.639 --> 00:11:46.370
Did you know about this massive war between
Apple, Google, and Microsoft?

